    "text": " Answered by: J., a 10-year-old who is fascinated by how things work. 1. How does a public water supply system work? The public water supply system is a network of pipes, pumps, and treatment plants that work together to bring clean water to your tap. Here's how it works: a) First, the water is collected from a source, like a river or a underground aquifer. b) The collected water is then taken to a treatment plant, where it is cleaned and purified to make it safe to drink. c) The treated water is then pumped into a network of underground pipes, called a distribution system. d) The water is then distributed to homes, schools, and businesses through a series of pipes that branch off from the main distribution system. e) Finally, the water is delivered to your tap, where you can use it to drink, cook, and clean. 2. How does a public power supply system work? The public power supply system is a network of power plants, transmission lines, and distribution lines that work together to bring electricity to your home. Here's how it works",
